**1. PDF Analysis:**
The provided PDF consists of two scientific papers detailing the development and characterization of Nano-11, a dendrimer-like alpha-D-glucan nanoparticle derived from sweet corn phytoglycogen. Nano-11 has a positive surface charge and a diameter of 70-80 nm, allowing it to efficiently adsorb negatively charged protein antigens. In vitro, Nano-11 is readily taken up by dendritic cells (DCs), leading to increased expression of costimulatory molecules and secretion of IL-1β and IL-12p40. In vivo, Nano-11 significantly enhances antibody responses to vaccine antigens, induces a transient local inflammatory response (predominantly monocytes/macrophages, few neutrophils), and efficiently delivers antigens to migratory DCs in draining lymph nodes. It demonstrates a better safety profile and less residual inflammation compared to aluminum adjuvants, positioning it as a promising, cost-effective vaccine adjuvant.

